# Output format

Emit `<comparch>` with these children, in this fixed order:
`<technical-specification>` → `<public-surface>` → `<private-surface>`
→ `<failure-surface>` → `<permissions>` → `<enforcement>` →
`<policies>` → `<dependencies>` → `<subcomponents>` →
`<sub-dependencies>` → `<references>` (optional).

**Cross-section consistency is what distinguishes a usable comparch
from one impl will fight.** The most common defect here is two
sections that disagree: an invariant claiming "X never happens"
alongside a failure-surface entry describing X happening; a
public-surface return type that cannot express a failure the failure
surface names; a primary-operation no surface entry mounts. Treat the
document as one artifact throughout generation, not sections you
reconcile at the end.

## Fragment sections

* `<technical-specification>` — role-level paragraphs (one concern per
  paragraph, blank-line separated, no bullets or headings): the
  concurrency model, the persistence pattern, the error-handling
  strategy, the testing approach for this component specifically. It
  propagates downward only and does not get regenerated when child
  impls iterate.
* `<public-surface>` — the only surface dependents will ever read.
  Types, function signatures, events, in fenced code blocks. Dependents
  need call shapes, return types, error modes, side-effect boundaries,
  and event contracts. Internal helpers belong in `<private-surface>`.
* `<private-surface>` — internal types and helpers visible to this
  component's own subcomponents, not to sibling dependents.
* `<failure-surface>` — residual risks that survive the invariants:
  coverage gaps, race windows, eventually-consistent behavior, wrong-
  output shapes the type system can't prevent. Not violations of
  invariants — if an entry contradicts one, fix the invariant (usually
  it's procedurally phrased and needs a structural rewrite) or the
  entry is fabricated. When the techspec commits to a typed-error
  discipline, every failure-surface entry needs a matching variant in
  the public surface's error union.

All four fragment sections are non-empty prose and code blocks only —
no nested XML.

## Permissions

`<permissions>` lists zero or more `<permission name="...">` with a
`<description>`, naming the compile-time permission atoms this
component's boundary enforces (docs/v5-design-decisions.md §2.9:
permissions are code, declared by the owning component). This is
taxonomy and semantics — which permissions exist and what they guard
— not the function→permission→role matrix, which is generated
elsewhere from extracted metadata and live rows, never hand-written
here.

## Enforcement

`<enforcement>` lists zero or more `<profile name="..."/>` naming
platform-defined enforcement profiles this scope is bound to (e.g.
`codegen:restricted` for review-pending compositions of
externally-vetted material, `purity:replay_floor` for event-sourced
reducer code). Name a profile only where this component's own
techspec or failure surface justifies it — an unbound profile reads as
policy nobody argued for.

## Policies

Zero or more component-local `<policy>`, same shape as sysarch's:
`<name>`, `<trigger>`, exactly one of `<required>resp_id</required>`
(the id must be one of this component's own top-level or subcomponent
responsibilities — cross-component references are a sysarch-level
policy, not this tier's) or `<structural/>` (scopes to this component
directly) or neither, then `<rationale>`.

## Dependencies (external)

`<dependencies>` lists sibling top-level components this component
reaches for, each `<dep to="..."/>` naming a real component id from
the input context. At most one per target; this is external only —
subcomponent-to-subcomponent deps live in `<sub-dependencies>`.

## Subcomponents

Each `<subcomponent alias="...">` has exactly one `<name>`,
`<purpose>`, `<owned-invariants>` (2 to 4, structural phrasing),
`<primary-operations>` (3 to 6), `<responsibilities>` (free-text prose,
1 to 3 sentences), `<owns>`, and `<implementation grade="real|
stubbed">` (required on every subcomponent — a stubbed grade further
carries `<swap>transparent|migration|reset</swap>` and `<exit-plan>`
prose stating what happens to stub-era data; the grade must be loud,
never assumed).

## The `<owns>` block

Each subcomp's `<owns>` declares which parent responsibilities it
claims a slice of and which specific feats of each:

```
<owns>
  <resp id="resp_payment01">
    <feat id="feat_payment_v01"/>
  </resp>
</owns>
```

**Default is single-owner.** Multi-owner is legal only for two seams:
a **read-path/write-path split** (a query subcomp and a mutation
subcomp co-owning a resp because the same feat manifests on both sides
of the data direction) or a **stage split** (subcomponents decomposing
along sequential processing stages of one flow, each stage co-claiming
the shared resp — name the stage in the free-text
`<responsibilities>` when you use this). Outside those two seams, do
not double-claim a resp — refactor the boundaries instead. Empty
`<owns/>` is legal for foundation/plumbing subcomponents that earn
their keep structurally rather than by anchoring a parent resp. Every
parent resp assigned to this component must be claimed by at least one
subcomponent, and every feat tagged on a claimed resp by at least one
subcomponent that claims it — coverage gaps are structural errors.

## Un-fanned-out components

Every component decomposes into at least one subcomponent in this
bundle — there is no "un-fanned-out" escape (docs/non-goals.md).

## Foundation subcomponent

If you decompose (always, per above), exactly one subcomponent carries
a self-closing `<foundation/>` marker, owning this component's root
territory. **Do not name it `Foundation`** — that name is reserved for
the project's top-level foundation component; use a name reflecting
this subcomponent's role inside the component (`AuthCore`,
`BillingRuntime`).

## Sub-dependencies

`<sub-dependencies>` lists edges between subcomponents, each
`<dep from="alias" to="alias"/>`; acyclic; every non-foundation
subcomponent depends on this component's foundation subcomponent.

## References

`<references>` (optional) lists `<reference target="ref_id"/>` for
any of the attached references above that this comparch actually
draws on.

# Final scan before emitting

* **Cross-section consistency.** Re-read the failure surface against
  every invariant and techspec promise, and against the public
  surface's return types.
* **Surface closure.** Every public-surface type, event, and field has
  a producing primary-operation; nothing in a public signature
  references a private-surface type.
* **Sibling contract discipline.** When you describe calling a
  sibling's operation, that operation must appear in the sibling's
  pubapi input above; if a sibling's pubapi looks skeletal (a
  sysarch-mint seed rather than an articulated surface), say so
  explicitly rather than inventing the call shape.
* **Single-owner default.** Any resp claimed by more than one
  subcomponent must fit one of the two named seams.
* **Rationale, not inventory.** Rewrite category-speak ("handles X",
  "manages Y") into concrete actions and distinctive rationale.

# Meta-rules

Do not include commentary about what you are doing. Output only the
`<comparch>` block.
